    {
      "slug": "the-pale-frontier",
      "title": "The Pale Frontier",
      "creator": "ErebusLich",
      "type": "world",
      "categories": [
        "worlds"
      ],
      "status": "refined",
      "date": "2026-03-30",
      "updated": "2026-05-02",
      "featured": true,
      "question": "consequences",
      "summary": "A lawless expanse beyond the City built around a collapsed orbital tether, where the Light behaves like weather rather than a resource and nothing you build is guaranteed to survive the season.",
      "cover": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "/media/concepts/c3.jpg",
        "thumbnail": "/media/concepts/c3.jpg"
      },
      "tools": [
        "Blender",
        "Photoshop"
      ],
      "body": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "heading": "The idea",
          "text": "The tether came down eighty years ago and the ice took the rest. What is left is a corridor of wreckage two hundred kilometres long, warm enough to live in only where the cable still holds a charge.\n\nLight here is not something you spend. It arrives, it moves, and it leaves, and the people who stayed learned to read it the way sailors read weather."
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "heading": "What this enables",
          "items": [
            "A destination that is dangerous for environmental reasons, not just enemy density",
            "Player structures that persist and decay between seasons",
            "Light as a forecast rather than a meter",
            "Factions defined by which stretch of cable they hold"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "timeline",
          "heading": "Timeline",
          "steps": [
            "The tether is built",
            "The Collapse",
            "The cable falls",
            "Eighty winters",
            "First Guardian arrival"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "takeaways": [
        "Weather is a better antagonist than another enemy faction",
        "Persistence matters more than size",
        "A destination should be legible from a single silhouette"
      ],
