    {
      "slug": "living-destinations",
      "title": "Living Destinations",
      "creator": "SystemSpecter",
      "type": "design",
      "categories": [
        "systems",
        "worlds"
      ],
      "status": "exploring",
      "date": "2026-04-18",
      "featured": true,
      "question": "consequences",
      "summary": "Destinations that change based on what players collectively do there, and stay changed. Territory won stays won until it is lost again.",
      "cover": {
        "type": "image",
        "media": "/media/concepts/c4.jpg",
        "thumbnail": "/media/concepts/c4.jpg"
      },
      "tools": [
        "Figma"
      ],
      "body": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "heading": "The idea",
          "text": "Public events reset on a timer because the world has no memory. Clear a Fallen camp and it is back in four minutes, which teaches players that nothing they do matters.\n\nGive each region a state that only changes when enough players push it, and hold that state for weeks rather than minutes."
        },
        {
          "type": "flow",
          "heading": "The proposal",
          "steps": [
            "Contested \u2014 Enemy presence high, patrols hostile",
            "Pushed \u2014 Players clear key positions",
            "Held \u2014 Vendors and fast travel appear",
            "Lost \u2014 Neglect or an enemy event reverses it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "heading": "Why this matters",
          "items": [
            "Effort accumulates instead of evaporating",
            "Gives lapsed players a reason to check in",
            "Creates natural stakes without PvP",
            "Seasonal story can respond to real player behaviour"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "takeaways": [
        "A world with memory makes small actions meaningful",
        "State should be slow enough to notice and fast enough to influence",
        "Losing ground has to be possible or holding it means nothing"
      ],
